\section{Model}{

\eqn{X = \text{score} - l \in \{0, \dots, m\}}, \eqn{m = u - l}, with
\eqn{E[X] = m\mu} and \eqn{\sigma^2 = m\mu(1 - \mu)[1 + (m - 1)\rho]}.
\eqn{\rho = 0} is the binomial, the least dispersed member of the family.
All moments are computed from the pmf. On a two-point scale (\eqn{m = 1})
scores are Bernoulli\eqn{(\mu)} whatever \eqn{\rho}, so \eqn{\rho} is not
identified and is returned as \code{NA}.
}

\section{Methods}{

For iid data with finite fourth moment, \eqn{E[S^2] = \sigma^2} and
\eqn{\mathrm{Var}(S^2) = \sigma^4 [2/(n-1) + \gamma_2/n]}.
\itemize{
\item \code{"chisq2"} (default): \eqn{S^2 \approx \sigma^2 \chi^2_\nu / \nu} with
\eqn{\nu = 2/[2/(n-1) + \gamma_2/n]} (Box / Satterthwaite).
\item \code{"chisq3"}: \eqn{S^2 \approx a + b\chi^2_\nu}, matching the first three
cumulants of \eqn{S^2}.
\item \code{"cf"}: Cornish-Fisher expansion of \eqn{S^2} using its third cumulant
\eqn{\kappa_3(S^2) = \kappa_6/n^2 + 12\kappa_2\kappa_4/(n(n-1)) +
  4(n-2)\kappa_3^2/(n(n-1)^2) + 8\kappa_2^3/(n-1)^2}.
\item \code{"exact"}: the exact distribution by dynamic programming (see
\code{\link[=s2_exact]{s2_exact()}}); small \code{n} only.
}
}

\section{Accuracy}{

The approximations are least accurate in the lower tail of a skewed score
distribution at small \code{n}. There the \code{"chisq3"} and \code{"cf"} lower bounds
can be anti-conservative: at \eqn{m = 63}, \code{mean = 2}, \code{n = 30},
\eqn{\rho = 0.1} (unconditional), about 2.6\% of samples fall below a
nominal 0.5\% lower bound. For \eqn{n \le 20}, use \code{method = "exact"}; above that, \code{"chisq2"}
with \code{condition_on_mean = TRUE}, whose upper bound is reliable. See
\verb{paper/sd-plausibility-bounds/} in the package sources.
}

\section{Conditioning on the mean}{

A reported SD comes with a reported mean. When the distribution is
skewed, \eqn{S^2} and \eqn{\bar X} are correlated
(\eqn{\mathrm{Cov}(\bar X, S^2) = \kappa_3/n}), so samples whose mean is
near \code{mean} have a narrower SD distribution than samples in general.
\code{condition_on_mean = TRUE} (the default) asks the question a check of a
reported (mean, SD) pair asks: \code{"chisq2"} uses the conditional variance
\eqn{\sigma^4[2/(n-1) + (\gamma_2 - \gamma_1^2)/n]}, and \code{"exact"}
conditions on the sums whose mean rounds to \code{mean} (at \code{digits_mean}
decimals). \code{"chisq3"} and \code{"cf"} need \code{condition_on_mean = FALSE}.
}

\section{Heterogeneity}{

With \code{tau > 0}, \eqn{\rho} is drawn from \eqn{N(\rho, \tau^2)} truncated to
\eqn{[0, 1)}, represented by \code{K} equal-weight quantile nodes. The bound is
the quantile of the mixture: the per-node CDFs are averaged, then
inverted. When \code{rho} and \code{tau} come from \code{\link[=rho_corpus_fit]{rho_corpus_fit()}}, consider
adding the prediction's squared standard error to \code{tau^2}.
}

